CREATED:20260819T131429Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260819T131429Z
UID:42743-1788980400-1788987600@www.torontomastergardeners.ca
SUMMARY:Garden disasters
DESCRIPTION:Every garden will eventually face some natural disasters and some human-made disasters. Learn what they are and how to combat\, manage or live in harmony with them. From Japanese beetles to squirrel invasions\, to fungal diseases\, tree issues\, and invasive plants\, we’ll cover the top ones and give you the strategies to face them head on. \nFor more information\, contact Riverdale Horticultural Society – https://riverdalehorticultural.ca/
URL:https://www.torontomastergardeners.ca/event/garden-disasters-2/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b4814c610f0038954eb9"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Laverne Cox & Jason Stanley: Identity\, Power and the Politics of 
 Our Time
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260909T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260909T200000
UID:6a85b4814c610f0038954eb9@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join actor and activist Laverne Cox and philosopher Jason Stan
 ley for a conversation about the political forces reshaping our world.\nAs
  civil rights\, historical truth and democratic norms come under pressure 
 in the United States\, Canadians are watching closely. What does it look l
 ike when a society begins to rewrite its past\, narrow who gets to belong 
 and chip away at hard-won freedoms? And what can communities everywhere—
 including here at home—learn from this moment?\nDrawing on Stanley's sch
 olarship on propaganda and historical memory and Cox's powerful voice as a
 n advocate for human dignity and equality\, this discussion moves past hea
 dlines to explore the bigger questions: how democracies erode\, how they'r
 e defended and why the stories a nation tells about itself matter so much.
 \nThoughtful\, urgent and ultimately hopeful\, this is a conversation for 
 anyone trying to make sense of the times we're living in.\nQ&A to follow. 
 Books available for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nLaverne Cox\n
 Jason Stanley\nRead more:\nTranscendent\nErasing History\nHow Fascism Work
 s\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm
 \, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket pol
 icy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest number of peopl
 e have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until 
 capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nBar:\nBeer/wine a
 vailable for purchase (debit/credit only).\n***\nThis event is part of our
  signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host local and international authors
 \, artists and thinkers in conversation about their new books and big idea
 s.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this 
 form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries
  Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be us
 ed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registran
 ts may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Ques
 tions about how the library handles your information can be directed to An
 swerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, To
 ronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b4814c610f0038954eb9
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Older Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8f14e760ccaf01c0212d9c"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_older_adults
SUMMARY:Nate Hendley: Mounties\, Marriage\, and Murder
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260909T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260909T200000
UID:6a8f14e760ccaf01c0212d9c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:John Wilson emigrated from Scotland to the Prairies in 1912\, 
 joined the Royal North-West Mounted Police (RNWMP) and fell in love with a
  Canadian woman named Jessie. Then\, his wife\, Mary\, whom he had abandon
 ed in Scotland along with his children\, unexpectedly showed up in Saskatc
 hewan. Mary soon disappeared and Wilson went from Mountie to murder suspec
 t.\nAuthor and freelance journalist\, Nate Hendley\, takes a look at a Can
 adian true-life murder mystery on the Prairies involving a double-crossing
  police officer\, providing a glimpse behind the carefully polished image 
 of the Mounties\, to reveal a duplicitous liar who tried to get away with 
 murder.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this 
 form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries
  Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be us
 ed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions
  about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answerl
 ine staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toro
 nto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a8f14e760ccaf01c0212d9c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Older Adults
END:VEVENT
