DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260918T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260918T140000
SUMMARY:The History of the Eaton Centre (NEW for 2026)
DESCRIPTION:Toronto History Walks\n**The Toronto History Walks Explorer Pa
 ss is available. Get it now for $125.00 for 1 year of unlimited tours\, em
 ail**\n**torontohistorywalks@gmail.com**\n**for more information or for si
 gning up!**\n\nMost people visit Toronto's Eaton Centre to shop\, and it's
  a great place to do just that. But few realize they are walking through o
 ne of the city's greatest stories of ambition\, controversy\, architecture
 \, and change.\n\nJoin me as we step beyond the storefronts to uncover the
  remarkable history hidden beneath the soaring glass roof of the galleria.
  Discover how this world famous complex rose from a maze of Victorian stre
 ets\, warehouses\, theatres\, and historic buildings\, and learn why one o
 f Toronto's largest redevelopment projects almost resulted in the demoliti
 on of Old City Hall and the beloved Church of the Holy Trinity.\n\nThis fa
 scinating walking tour explores nearly 160 years of Toronto history\, begi
 nning with the extraordinary rise of Timothy Eaton and the department stor
 e that transformed shopping into the "art of shopping\," almost overnight.
 \n\nWe'll examine the neighbourhood that existed before the Eaton Centre\,
  meet some of the colourful characters who worked and lived here\, and tra
 ce the dramatic battles between developers\, preservationists\, politician
 s\, and citizens that forever changed the face of downtown Toronto. Along 
 the way\, you'll see surviving landmarks\, forgotten streets\, hidden arch
 itectural secrets\, and public art that millions pass every year without n
 oticing.\n\nYou'll also discover why the Eaton Centre became an internatio
 nal model for shopping centre design\, admire its spectacular vaulted glas
 s ceiling\, and hear the stories behind the famous flock of suspended Cana
 da geese.\n\nWe'll explore how the complex evolved through the decline of 
 the Eaton's department store\, and the arrival of new retailers\, that hel
 ped redefine the downtown core. From engineering marvels to architectural 
 triumphs\, this is the story of how a shopping mall became one of Canada's
  most recognizable landmarks.\n\nWhether you're a lifelong Torontonian\, a
  first-time visitor\, a lover of architecture\, or someone fascinated by u
 rban history\, this tour offers a new perspective on a place you thought y
 ou knew. By the end of our walk\, you'll never look at the Eaton Centre th
 e same way again\, I promise. Come and discover the hidden history beneath
  the glass canopy\, and experience one of Toronto's most compelling storie
 s.\n\nThis event is for all levels and walkers\, there are a few steps her
 e and there to navigate\, but mostly on one level.\n\nThis walking tour be
 gins on the steps of Old City Hall at the northeast corner of Bay Street a
 nd Queen Street West\, and\nhas a ticket price of $15.00 per person.\n\n**
 Please note: Please e-transfer the ticket amount when booking your space f
 or this tour! You can e-transfer to the following email at:**\n**torontohi
 storywalks@gmail.com**
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/the-history-of-parkdale/events/315569
 432/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260706T115939Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260706T115939Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b74f4c610f0038954f4a"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Ghost Stories of Toronto: Colonial Spectres
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260918T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260918T130000
UID:6a85b74f4c610f0038954f4a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:"It’s only by our lack of ghosts we’re haunted\," the poet
  Earle Birney once said of Canada. But the truth is that people have been 
 telling supernatural tales in Toronto since the city was founded\, and lon
 g before that\, too. We'll begin this series of talks with author Adam Bun
 ch by heading back to the city's founding days as a settlement to explore 
 ghost stories from the early 1800s and see what they have to reveal about 
 colonialism\, the War of 1812\, and the fears of the settlers who lived in
  this place two hundred years ago.\nAbout this event's guest:\nAdam Bunch\
 nRead More:\nThe Toronto Book of Love\nThe Toronto Book of the Dead\nAbout
  this series:\nYou can learn a lot about Toronto through its ghost stories
 . Whether you believe in the supernatural or not\, the tales we tell about
  spirits and spectres illuminate the true history of the city. They're oft
 en tied to dramatic stories of death and disaster\, to fascinating histori
 c sites\, and to pivotal moments that helped shape our communities. In thi
 s series of talks by author Adam Bunch\, we'll explore Toronto's past thro
 ugh tales of ghosts\, séances and hauntings. Whether it's phantom horses 
 from the War of 1812\, the strange prime minister who believed in the occu
 lt\, or the ghost who was arrested by the Toronto Police Morality Squad\, 
 these supernatural stories have a lot to teach us about the history of the
  place we call home.\n***\nImportant information for accessing this live v
 irtual event:\nYou must register for a free ticket via Eventbrite to gain 
 access to this event.\nThis event will be presented in a Zoom Webinar. Onc
 e registered\, you will receive reminder emails from both Zoom and Eventbr
 ite with links and instructions to access your unique Zoom Webinar link to
  watch this event.\nAt the time of the event\, you must be signed in to Zo
 om or Eventbrite with the same email address that you used to book your ti
 cket in order to access your unique event link. Unique event links can onl
 y be open on one device at a time.\n***\nAccessibility at Toronto Public L
 ibrary\nWe are committed to making our content accessible to as many Toron
 tonians as possible. Please call or email us if you are Deaf or have a dis
 ability and would like to request accommodation to participate in this pro
 gram. Please let us know as far in advance as possible and we will do our 
 best to meet your request. At least three weeks' notice is preferred. Phon
 e 416-393-7099 or email accessibleservices@tpl.ca\n***\nInformation Collec
 tion Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the To
 ronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(
 c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program re
 gistration\, delivery and evaluation.\nRegistrants may be contacted to fil
 l out a brief survey following the program\n. Questions about how the libr
 ary handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answer
 line@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b74f4c610f0038954f4a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
