SUMMARY:Let's Talk Depression: What It Is\, What It Isn't\, and What Helps
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T200000
UID:6a6bcaece30fe4845965bdc1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Depression is one of the most common mental health conditions 
 in the world\; yet it remains widely misunderstood.\nFar more than ordinar
 y sadness or "feeling down\," clinical depression (also called major depre
 ssion) is a complex mood disorder shaped by a combination of factors\, inc
 luding genetic predisposition\, changes in brain chemistry\, life stress\,
  and personal history. For many people\, it can be debilitating\, persiste
 nt\, and difficult to recognize — both in ourselves and in those we care
  about.\nThis talk will focus on demystifying depression and offer practic
 al\, evidence-informed insights into understanding and addressing it.\nWha
 t to Expect:\nUnderstanding Depression:\nThis talk will explore what depre
 ssion actually is (and what it is not)\, and how to recognize when low moo
 d may be something more serious.\nDispelling Misinformation:\nAddressing c
 ommon myths and misconceptions that can prevent or delay people from seeki
 ng help.\nGetting Help:\nA discussion of practical ways to support ourselv
 es and others\, what to do when someone close to us is struggling\, and wh
 en it may be time to seek professional care.\nAbout Dr. Gerasimos Konstant
 inou\nDr. Gerasimos Konstantinou is a psychiatrist and researcher based in
  Toronto\, where he works at the Centre for Addiction and Mental Health (C
 AMH) and Toronto Western Hospital. He specializes in mood disorders\, part
 icularly depression\, and in advanced treatment approaches for people who 
 have not found relief through standard medications or therapy.\nHe is curr
 ently an Assistant Professor in the Department of Psychiatry at the Univer
 sity of Toronto\, an Associate Scientist at CAMH's Campbell Family Mental 
 Health Research Institute\, and leads the Mood Disorders Clinic at CAMH\, 
 which supports people facing acute episodes of depression and anxiety.\nTh
 is program is presented by Toronto Public Library in collaboration with\nT
 he Centre for Addiction and Mental Health (CAMH)\n.\nYour Health Matters\n
 This program is part of Toronto Public Library’s\nYour Health Matters\ni
 nitiative\, which offers trusted health programs and resources to help peo
 ple make informed decisions about their health. Learn more by exploring th
 e\nYour Health Matters web page\n.\nReserve Your Spot Today!\nThis virtual
  Zoom event is free to join but registration is required. Once registered\
 , you’ll receive an email with the Zoom link.\nWe can’t wait to see yo
 u there!
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a6bcaece30fe4845965bdc1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Toronto Public Library \u2014 Adults": "https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85a0792f41840bbfc30570"}
X-SOURCE-ID:tpl_adults
SUMMARY:Club de lecture de Brentwood
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260921T200000
UID:6a85a0792f41840bbfc30570@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Aimez-vous lire en français? Avez-vous envie de participer à
  des discussions autour des livres dans une ambiance conviviale et déten
 due?\nRejoignez notre club de lecture en français! Venez échanger vos po
 ints de vue\, analyser ce qui vous a marqué avec le regard d'autres lecte
 urs\, et faire de belles découvertes littéraires.\nOn se réunit le 21 s
 eptembre pour découvrir\n« Moi qui n'ai pas connu les hommes » de\nJa
 cqueline Harpman\nLe livre sera disponible en prêt à la bibliothèque\nB
 rentwood\n\, au comptoir de prêt\, à partir du 21 août.\nSi vous voulez
  en savoir plus sur nos clubs de lectures\, veuillez nous envoyer un courr
 iel à\nprogrammesenfrancais@tpl.ca\nVeuillez noter que ce programme s’a
 dresse aux personnes francophones ainsi qu’à celles ayant un niveau ava
 ncé en français. Il est nécessaire de pouvoir lire\, discuter et critiq
 uer un livre avec une aisance de communication en français.\nSi vous souh
 aitez pratiquer votre français mais que vous avez plutôt un niveau débu
 tant ou intermédiaire\, nous vous encourageons à découvrir nos cercles 
 de conversation.\nLe sous-titrage en direct ou automatique\, l’interpré
 tation en langue des signes\, les services d’intervenant\, ou les servic
 es d’accompagnement sont disponibles sur demande. Veuillez soumettre vot
 re demande au moins deux semaines à l’avance à :\ntpl.ca/accessibility
 -events\n.
LOCATION:Brentwood
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85a0792f41840bbfc30570
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
