BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Meetup Toronto Postgres": "https://www.meetup.com/toronto-postgres/events/316166689/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:meetup_toronto_postgres
X-SOURCE:Meetup: Toronto Postgres
UID:event_316166689@meetup.com
SEQUENCE:1
DTSTAMP:20260905T042022Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260922T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260922T160000
SUMMARY:Postgres as a Coordinator
DESCRIPTION:Toronto Postgres\nTrusting Postgres to store data is one thing
 \; trusting it to coordinate work is another. This talk walks through the 
 design we settled on — commit the durable fact inside the transaction\, 
 fire side effects after commit\, and rebuild the job queue from committed 
 rows so the chain is always the source of truth — and the real failure m
 odes we hit getting there: an advisory-lock self-deadlock that Postgres's 
 own detector couldn't see (half the wait cycle lived in the application)\,
  pg-boss semantics that didn't mean what we assumed\, and MVCC read-then-w
 rite races. For each: the reproduction\, the root cause\, and the structur
 al guard that keeps it from recurring. Because AI agents wrote and tested 
 much of this\, the talk also covers where that helped\, where it quietly d
 idn't\, and the testing discipline we adopted as a result.\nWhat you'll ta
 ke away:\n\n* A durable coordinator pattern: commit-in-transaction\, side-
 effects-after-commit\, queue reconstructable from committed rows\n* Concre
 te concurrency failure modes on Postgres — advisory-lock scope\, pg-boss
  singleton/priority semantics\, MVCC visibility — and how to design them
  out\n* Why the structural guard\, not the one-off fix\, is what makes cor
 rectness stick\n* What building and testing with AI agents got right\, and
  what needed guardrails
URL;VALUE=URI:https://www.meetup.com/toronto-postgres/events/316166689/
STATUS:CONFIRMED
CREATED:20260818T130905Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260818T130905Z
CLASS:PUBLIC
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Philosophy": "https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/2026-jerome-s-simon-memorial-lectures-barbara-vetter-freie-universitat-berlin/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:philosophy
X-SOURCE:UofT Philosophy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260922T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260924T170000
DTSTAMP:20260828T140200Z
CREATED:20260817T162046Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260828T140200Z
UID:35331-1790089200-1790269200@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:2026 Jerome S. Simon Memorial Lectures (Barbara Vetter\, Freie Universität Berlin)
DESCRIPTION:We are pleased to announce Barbara Vetter as our esteemed speaker for the 2026 Jerome S. Simon Memorial Lectures. Dr. Vetter is a professor of theoretical philosophy at Freie Universität Berlin\, and co-director of the “Human Abilities” project (www.human-abilities.de). Her work focuses on the metaphysics and epistemology of modal properties — dispositions\, abilities\, and so on — and their role in the metaphysics and epistemology of modality. She published Potentiality: From Dispositions to Modality with the Oxford University Press in 2015. In 2026\, she received a Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz prize.  Dr. Vetter is currently working on a book that develops an agency-based approach to the epistemology of modality. \nThe title of the series is “Modal Knowledge in Action.” \nMore information on the three lectures to follow.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/2026-jerome-s-simon-memorial-lectures-barbara-vetter-freie-universitat-berlin/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building 100
CATEGORIES:Alumni,Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/Barbara-Vetter-325-x-220.png
END:VEVENT
