SUMMARY:Reaping What She Sows: Women at the Forefront of Toronto Food Syst
 ems Change
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261010T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261010T163000
UID:6a95d6d21c197d11325c2d52@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Global events - from pandemics\, war\, and foodborne illnesses
  - have threatened farmers' livelihoods\, created supermarket shortages\, 
 and revealed to consumers that our food system is broken. In the face of t
 hese threats to our well-being and food security\, what can we do as a co
 mmunity to strengthen our local food system?\nMeet\nNancy Matsumoto\n\, au
 thor of\nReaping What She Sows\n: How Women Are Rebuilding Our Broken Food
  System\n\, who will introduce her book and highlight Toronto women chang
 emakers building up local and regional supply chains to combat the destruc
 tive effects of Big Food.\nPaul DeCampo\n\, representative of Karma Co-op 
 Grocery Store will moderate the discussion\, which will include the follow
 ing guest panelists:\nJenn Pfenning\nfrom Pfennings Organic Farm\nSabrina
  Banh\nfrom Fisherfolk\nTogether\, they will discuss the importance of a 
 resilient\, transparent\, climate-friendly local and regional food system
 —for our health\, the health of workers\, animals and communities\, and 
 the future of our planet. Attendees will be inspired to learn how they can
  also help create positive food system change and contribute to our region
 al food security.\nPresented in celebration of Women's History Month\, as 
 part of Small Business Month\, and as part of TPL's environmental program
  series\,\nOur Fragile Planet\n\, supported by Friends of Toronto Public 
 Library\, South Chapter.\nFree program. Please register. The presentation
  will include an audience Q&A.\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto Public
  Library:\nReaping What She Sows\n: How Women Are Rebuilding Our Broken Fo
 od System\n(book)\nReaping What She Sows\nReaping What She Sows\nReaping W
 hat She Sows\n(ebook)\nBio\nNancy Matsumoto\n\, an award-winning freelance
  writer and editor who writes about agroecology\, food sovereignty\, food\
 , drink\, and Japanese American culture and history.\nPaul DeCampo\nhas wo
 rked as an educator\, food researcher and wine marketer. A Karma Co-op Gro
 cery Store member since 1997\, Paul has served in various volunteer roles
  at this community-owned\, co-operative grocery store\, including on the B
 oard of Directors and as General Manager.\nInformation Collection Notice:\
 nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public 
 Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d
 ). The information will only be used for event and program registration\, 
 delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your info
 rmation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-
 397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.\nReaping What She Sows
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95d6d21c197d11325c2d52
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
