SUMMARY:Wellness Recovery Action Plan (WRAP)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T143000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T173000
UID:6a62ab769e0a050e3e5416e4@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:WRAP is for\nanyone\nlooking to strengthen their well-being—
 whether you’re supporting your mental health\, managing stress\, or simp
 ly wanting to feel more balanced and resilient in daily life. Participant
 s will receive a\nWRAP certificate\nupon program completion.\nIn an 8-wee
 k WRAP group\, individuals explore:\nself-care tools like peer connections
 \, relaxation and mindfulness techniques\, stress reduction strategies\, a
 nd more\nresources for keeping well and feeling better in difficult times.
 \nInterested in learning more? Drop in for the information session on\nSep
 tember 3\, 2:30-4:30 pm\n.\nThis WRAP Group meets\nevery Thursday\,\nonce
  a week for 8 weeks\, September 10 to October 29\n. Registration closes af
 ter week 3\, or when full.\nTTC Presto tickets will be provided.\nSnacks a
 nd refreshments are provided for all participants. Ingredient lists will b
 e available at the program.\nThis program is in collaboration with\nGerst
 ein Crisis Centre's WRAP initiative\n. Gerstein Crisis Centre is solely re
 sponsible for the protection of \npersonal information collected through t
 he program registration \nprocess.
LOCATION:College/Shaw\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a62ab769e0a050e3e5416e4
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Philosophy": "https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/history-of-philosophy-research-group-talk-bridger-ehli-iu-bloomington/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:philosophy
X-SOURCE:UofT Philosophy
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261015T170000
DTSTAMP:20260828T160545Z
CREATED:20260828T160545Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260828T160545Z
UID:35617-1792076400-1792083600@philosophy.utoronto.ca
SUMMARY:History of Philosophy Research Group Talk (Bridger Ehli\, IU-Bloomington)
DESCRIPTION:The History of Modern Philosophy Group is pleased to welcome as its guest speaker Bridger Ehli\, an assistant professor in the Department of Philosophy at the University of Indiana – Bloomington. His research concerns themes in epistemology\, metaphysics\, and the philosophy of mind in early modern European philosophy. He is especially interested in early modern discussions of skepticism\, especially as they relate to questions about mental representation\, concepts\, and the psychology of belief. Most of his work has focused on Hume’s contributions to these issues. In a forthcoming book\, he argues that much of Hume’s skepticism arises from his thinking about the limits of intelligibility rather than those of justified belief. \nDetails to follow.
URL:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/event/history-of-philosophy-research-group-talk-bridger-ehli-iu-bloomington/
LOCATION:Jackman Humanities Building\, Room 418\, 170 St. George Street\, Toronto\, Ontario\, M5R 2M8\, Canada
CATEGORIES:Graduate,St. George
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/png:https://philosophy.utoronto.ca/wp-content/uploads/ehli_bridger_225-x-225.png
END:VEVENT
